{
  "note": "Cavendish Crescent in 1891, from the pages A. Hagues sent. Deliberately filed WITHOUT a property, so every household lands on the unfiled page for him to place — because the record holds a Cavendish Crescent North AND a South at 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 15, and the census pages say only 'Cavendish Crescent'.\n\nThe two image sets are two different sides of the street, and the numbers prove it: L16782 runs 15, 13, 7, 5, 3, 1 and L16848_4 runs 5, 7, 9. Both contain a 5 and a 7, and two households cannot share an address. So the side is one decision per page, not nine decisions per household — and each address below names its page so the two number 5s and two number 7s stay apart on the unfiled page instead of merging into one group.\n\nThe one piece of evidence bearing on which side is which: Alfred Goater and the two Barnett visitors, at Cavendish Lodge on the L16848 page, are already in the record at 3 Cavendish Crescent NORTH (#22) for 1901. That points to L16848 being the north side, but it is a pointer and not a proof, and nothing here assumes it.\n\nTWO HOUSEHOLDS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT. 9 Cavendish Crescent, the Thomas Bayley household, is already complete in the record for 1891 — Thomas (#1), Annie (#2), Hester Theodora (#9), Muriel Katherine (filed under Huskinson, #6) and the servants Whysall (#16), Murden (#17), Watson (#18), Johnson (#19), Stevenson (#20), Simpson (#21), Dannicliffe (#22) and Shipmore (#23). Importing it would duplicate twelve people. Note that it sits at property #9, which resolves to 14 Barrack Lane — a misfiling worth correcting, but a reassignment rather than an import. And Cavendish Lodge's three people are bound by id below so their 1891 is added to the people already held, not created afresh.\n\nAges are as the enumerator wrote them, so a birth year worked back from one may be a year out either way.",
